pe = (2-1)/(3-2) = 1, sunny.\n- Anchor (2,2): slope = (2-2)/(3-2) = 0, not sunny.\n- Anchor (2,3): slope = (2-3)/(3-2) = -1, not sunny.\n- Anchor (2,4): slope = (2-4)/(3-2) = -2, sunny.\n\nThus (2,1) or (2,4) can be paired with (3,2) to give sunny lines.\n\nThus we need to assign anchors accordingly.\n\nWe have 4 anchors: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4). We need to cover (5,1), (4,1), (4,2), (3,1), (3,2), (3,3). That's 6 points. Each line can cover at most 2 of them (since each line anchored at a column 2 point can intersect at most one other column). So we need to pair each anchor with up to two points (including its anchor). Actually each line already covers its anchor (2,b). So each line covers at least its anchor, plus possibly one other point in another column. So each line can cover at most 2 points (anchor + another). So total coverage capacity is 4 anchors + up to 4 other points = up to 8 points. We need to cover 6 other points (excluding anchors). So we need to assign each of the 6 points to some line (maybe some lines cover two of them). Since each line can cover at most one extra point, we need at least 6 lines to cover 6 extra points if each line covers only one extra point. But we have only 4 lines, so some lines must cover two extra points? But a line cannot intersect two other columns at integer points because it would need to pass through three points (anchor and two other points) which would be collinear. However, it's possible if the three points are collinear, i.e., the anchor and two other points lie on the same line. That would be a line passing through three points in different columns. Is that possible? Let's check if there exists a line that passes through (2,b) and (3,c) and (4,d) all integer points. That would be a line with slope (c-b)/(3-2) = c-b, and also (d-c)/(4-3) = d-c must equal same slope. So we need c-b = d-c => d = 2c - b. Also need to satisfy that points are within T_n constraints: c ≤ n+1-3 = n-2, d ≤ n+1-4 = n-3. For n=5, constraints: